WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:03.360
Grandpa says the meat's too tough to chew.

2
00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:05.410
So switch him to congee.

3
00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:08.780
Softer food, problem solved, right?

4
00:00:08.911 --> 00:00:11.411
The research says it's not that simple.

5
00:00:20.064 --> 00:00:23.054
Taiwan is now a super-aged society.

6
00:00:23.064 --> 00:00:24.944
How well older people eat

7
00:00:24.954 --> 00:00:27.413
shapes their nutrition and their health.

8
00:00:27.423 --> 00:00:29.373
Age, illness and lost teeth

9
00:00:29.383 --> 00:00:32.013
slowly wear down our ability to chew.

10
00:00:32.023 --> 00:00:34.803
Many older people switch to softer food

11
00:00:34.813 --> 00:00:36.640
without even noticing.

12
00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:38.330
By the time they lose weight

13
00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:40.120
or their nutrition slips,

14
00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:41.740
the problem has usually been there

15
00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:43.070
for a while.

16
00:00:44.452 --> 00:00:45.722
So the first step is

17
00:00:45.732 --> 00:00:48.172
to measure how well someone can chew.

18
00:00:48.197 --> 00:00:49.777
A Japanese study compared

19
00:00:49.787 --> 00:00:51.407
four chewing tests.

20
00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:53.137
By the original standard,

21
00:00:53.147 --> 00:00:55.847
more than 8 in 10 older hospital patients

22
00:00:55.857 --> 00:00:57.097
chewed poorly.

23
00:00:57.107 --> 00:00:58.757
With an adjusted standard,

24
00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:01.037
that fell to 54%.

25
00:01:02.018 --> 00:01:05.378
In Taiwan, a study of 1,000 older adults

26
00:01:05.388 --> 00:01:06.828
living at home found

27
00:01:06.838 --> 00:01:09.388
that about 1 in 4 chewed poorly.

28
00:01:09.398 --> 00:01:11.428
And how well they thought they chewed

29
00:01:11.438 --> 00:01:13.888
matched their test results closely.

30
00:01:14.877 --> 00:01:17.817
Another study, of 100 older adults,

31
00:01:17.827 --> 00:01:19.477
also found that a questionnaire

32
00:01:19.487 --> 00:01:22.767
and a real chewing test agreed closely.

33
00:01:23.321 --> 00:01:25.641
So a simple questionnaire can pick out

34
00:01:25.651 --> 00:01:27.911
the people who need a closer look.

35
00:01:29.359 --> 00:01:31.179
It also matters what kind of teeth

36
00:01:31.189 --> 00:01:33.689
do the chewing, not only how many.

37
00:01:33.699 --> 00:01:36.309
People who bite with removable dentures

38
00:01:36.319 --> 00:01:37.989
chewed far worse than people

39
00:01:37.999 --> 00:01:40.909
with natural teeth or fixed dentures.

40
00:01:40.919 --> 00:01:42.599
They did about as well as people

41
00:01:42.609 --> 00:01:45.309
whose back teeth don't meet at all.

42
00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:49.317
When chewing gets hard,

43
00:01:49.327 --> 00:01:51.097
what happens to the plate?

44
00:01:51.107 --> 00:01:52.727
A Korean study looked at

45
00:01:52.737 --> 00:01:55.847
more than 7,800 older adults.

46
00:01:55.857 --> 00:01:57.057
Those who found chewing hard

47
00:01:57.067 --> 00:01:59.697
ate less grain, potato, fruit,

48
00:01:59.707 --> 00:02:01.137
meat and dairy.

49
00:02:01.147 --> 00:02:03.687
They ate about a quarter less fresh fruit,

50
00:02:03.697 --> 00:02:04.687
and they were more likely

51
00:02:04.697 --> 00:02:06.377
to be malnourished.

52
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:08.840
Dentures help.

53
00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:11.110
In a study of more than 600 people

54
00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:13.050
in Japanese care homes,

55
00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:14.630
those who wore dentures

56
00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.070
had 2.82 times the odds

57
00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:19.250
of staying on a regular diet.

58
00:02:21.812 --> 00:02:22.832
So what sits between

59
00:02:22.842 --> 00:02:24.652
teeth and nutrition?

60
00:02:24.673 --> 00:02:26.653
Data from more than 1,700

61
00:02:26.663 --> 00:02:29.533
nursing home residents in Hangzhou

62
00:02:29.543 --> 00:02:30.413
showed that people

63
00:02:30.423 --> 00:02:32.923
with 20 or more natural teeth

64
00:02:32.933 --> 00:02:35.283
ate a wider range of foods,

65
00:02:35.293 --> 00:02:36.993
and were better nourished.

66
00:02:37.973 --> 00:02:40.143
More teeth means more variety,

67
00:02:40.153 --> 00:02:42.967
and more variety means better nutrition.

68
00:02:42.977 --> 00:02:45.837
The mouth also shapes what people choose.

69
00:02:45.847 --> 00:02:48.027
At a nursing home in Taiwan,

70
00:02:48.037 --> 00:02:49.677
residents picked their own cookie

71
00:02:49.687 --> 00:02:51.737
from three levels of hardness.

72
00:02:51.747 --> 00:02:53.277
The worse their mouths,

73
00:02:53.287 --> 00:02:55.837
the more often they took the softest one.

74
00:02:58.279 --> 00:02:59.629
And the same food

75
00:02:59.639 --> 00:03:01.965
isn't equally hard for everyone.

76
00:03:01.975 --> 00:03:04.175
In Korea, 32 older women

77
00:03:04.185 --> 00:03:07.465
tasted radish kimchi, from soft to firm.

78
00:03:07.475 --> 00:03:09.095
The women with full dentures

79
00:03:09.105 --> 00:03:11.125
chewed with less muscle force

80
00:03:11.135 --> 00:03:13.075
and pressed with weaker tongues.

81
00:03:13.085 --> 00:03:15.835
They also found the firmest kimchi harder

82
00:03:15.845 --> 00:03:18.195
than the women with their own teeth did.

83
00:03:19.238 --> 00:03:20.558
But like everyone else,

84
00:03:20.568 --> 00:03:22.778
they liked the plain kimchi best,

85
00:03:22.788 --> 00:03:24.808
the kind that hadn't been blanched.

86
00:03:24.818 --> 00:03:26.708
They just couldn't chew it well.

87
00:03:29.181 --> 00:03:30.861
Struggling to chew a food

88
00:03:30.871 --> 00:03:33.021
doesn't mean you stop wanting it.

89
00:03:34.803 --> 00:03:35.903
In Singapore,

90
00:03:35.913 --> 00:03:38.813
among 100 older adults living at home,

91
00:03:38.823 --> 00:03:41.703
those with chewing or swallowing problems

92
00:03:41.713 --> 00:03:43.213
liked much the same foods

93
00:03:43.223 --> 00:03:44.713
as everyone else.

94
00:03:46.721 --> 00:03:49.441
The link may also run the other way.

95
00:03:49.451 --> 00:03:52.876
A survey of more than 1,200 people in Taiwan

96
00:03:52.886 --> 00:03:54.956
found that the more poor eating habits

97
00:03:54.966 --> 00:03:55.926
they had,

98
00:03:55.936 --> 00:03:58.086
the worse their oral health.

99
00:04:00.255 --> 00:04:01.545
One caution:

100
00:04:01.555 --> 00:04:03.685
most of these studies are snapshots,

101
00:04:03.695 --> 00:04:06.035
taken at a single point in time.

102
00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:07.295
They show a link,

103
00:04:07.305 --> 00:04:08.265
but they can't prove

104
00:04:08.275 --> 00:04:10.155
which one causes the other.

105
00:04:10.933 --> 00:04:12.503
In practice, start with

106
00:04:12.513 --> 00:04:14.933
a simple questionnaire to check chewing.

107
00:04:14.943 --> 00:04:17.453
Keep natural teeth as long as you can,

108
00:04:17.463 --> 00:04:19.703
or get dentures that fit well.

109
00:04:20.322 --> 00:04:21.452
As for the food,

110
00:04:21.462 --> 00:04:24.392
work with a dietitian to adjust its texture,

111
00:04:24.402 --> 00:04:26.322
so older people can chew it,

112
00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:28.492
and still enjoy eating it.
